    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How Does the Government Support Communities?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: justify;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret dialogues from various social contexts. Interactive: Highlight opinions, both positive and negative, related to an audio or video recording, during discussions.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret perspectives in dialogues on volunteer work (e.g., "In Panama, volunteer programs such as disaster relief initiatives help during emergencies, but some believe the government should allocate more resources for long-term infrastructure.") Can highlight positive and negative opinions in audio recordings about community support (e.g., "Some community members believe we should increase support for Basura Cero initiatives, while others argue that more focus is needed on improving public transportation.") Can ask questions to clarify viewpoints in community discussions (e.g., "What have the local authorities been doing to improve services for residents?" "The local authorities have been expanding public transportation.")
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 40%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 40%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting various community issues (e.g., uncollected trash, lack of public transportation, underserved areas) and ongoing community projects (e.g., volunteer brigades cleaning parks, new community centers, disaster relief initiatives). Students work in pairs to discuss what they see and infer the issues and potential solutions. The teacher then asks guiding questions: "What problems do these pictures suggest our communities might face? Who typically takes responsibility for addressing these issues?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words relevant to the theme (e.g., <i>government, resources, volunteers, projects, allocate, improve, underserved, initiative</i>). The teacher models the pronunciation of each word, focusing on syllable stress (e.g., 'GOV-ern-ment', 're-SOURCE-es', 'VOL-un-teers', 'UN-der-served'). The teacher then models sentences integrating passive voice and present perfect continuous, e.g., "Community programs <b>are funded by the government</b>" and "<b>Volunteers have been working</b> to improve public services." The teacher emphasizes content word stress in these sentences to improve rhythm and fluency.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and modeled structures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a project is 'funded by the government', who is giving the money?" (Expected answer: The government.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'volunteers have been working to clean up the park,' are they finished or are they still doing it?" (Expected answer: They are still working or recently finished and the impact is still felt.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio recording of a short panel discussion (approx. 2-3 minutes) from a local radio program, featuring two community leaders and a government representative discussing the impact of community support initiatives and the government's role. The dialogue includes positive and negative opinions on current programs and uses target vocabulary and grammatical structures (passive voice, modals for necessity, present perfect continuous). The register is semi-formal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret dialogues from various social contexts; highlight opinions, both positive and negative, related to an audio recording.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once and identify the main topic of the discussion. Teacher asks: "What are the speakers mainly talking about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (Specific Information):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise or a matching task. For example: "Match each speaker to the main point they make." or "Determine if the following statements about community support programs are True or False based on the audio." (e.g., Statement 1: "More resources are being allocated to education programs." Statement 2: "Volunteers have been crucial in providing shelter during emergencies.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying specific details and opinions in dialogues. Target grammar: Passive voice for support structures ("Community programs are funded by the government."), Modals for necessity ("Individuals should contribute."), Present perfect continuous for detailed descriptions ("Volunteers have been working"). Target vocabulary: <i>resources, services, responsibilities, volunteers, allocate, improve, promote, contribute, underserved, impactful, essential</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Listening):</b> Students receive a transcript of parts of the audio with key vocabulary words, passive voice verbs, or present perfect continuous phrases missing. They listen to the audio a third time and fill in the blanks. Example: "Many local _____ (programs) are _____ (funded) by city hall." "_____ (Volunteers) _____ (have been working) tirelessly for the clean-up initiative."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Opinion Identification):</b> The teacher plays specific segments of the audio. After each segment, students identify whether the opinion expressed is positive or negative regarding government support or community involvement. They underline the words or phrases that indicate the sentiment. (e.g., "The local authorities have been expanding public transportation." - Positive. "Some residents feel more focus is needed on improving public services." - Negative). Teacher reminds students to focus on stressed content words in their own speech when discussing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Opinion Exchange & Clarification):</b> Students work in pairs. They listen to 2-3 short clips from the audio again. One student identifies a positive opinion and explains why, while the other identifies a negative one. They then ask questions to clarify viewpoints (Pragmatic skill). E.g., "What specifically did the speaker say about the government's support for underserved areas?" or "Could you give an example of how community involvement has been important for this project?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion on Community Support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form small groups (3-4 students). The teacher presents a new short audio prompt (e.g., a news report about a specific local community project, such as a "Basura Cero initiative" or a "volunteer brigade" building a new "shelter"). Students listen actively and take notes on the various opinions and facts presented regarding the project, paying attention to the use of passive voice, modals, and present perfect continuous. After listening, groups engage in a collaborative discussion (Sociolinguistic skill). They must identify and discuss:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What positive and negative opinions were expressed about the initiative?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How has the local government been encouraging more people to volunteer in this community? (Pragmatic skill)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What responsibilities should individuals take regarding such community projects?</li>
                </ul>
                Students are encouraged to use the target vocabulary, grammar structures, and practice stressing content words to improve fluency during their discussions. (e.g., "The project <b>is funded by</b> local donations," "More <b>resources should be allocated</b>," "Local residents <b>have been organizing</b> clean-up drives.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the small group discussions (Performance Stage), the teacher circulates and uses an observation checklist to assess students' ability to interpret dialogues, highlight opinions, and use the target language effectively in discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interprets Perspectives:</b> Accurately identifies and explains both positive and negative opinions from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Uses Target Language:</b> Appropriately integrates passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous, as well as target vocabulary (<i>resources, services, allocate, improve, underserved</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation & Fluency:</b> Intelligible pronunciation with appropriate stress on content words; contributes to the discussion with a reasonable flow of speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Clarification:</b> Actively participates in group discussion, asks clarifying questions, and responds to peers' viewpoints.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel in identifying different opinions in an audio recording about community support?" (Scale of 1-5)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one new vocabulary word or grammar structure you used effectively today in your discussion."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned today about how the government supports communities, or how communities support themselves, through listening?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific listening strategy did I find most helpful today (e.g., listening for keywords, identifying tone, focusing on opinions) and why?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the listening activities worked well in helping students interpret opinions and use the target language? (e.g., identifying positive/negative opinions, using passive voice to describe support structures). What evidence do I have?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with listening comprehension or expressing their views? (e.g., more pre-listening vocabulary support, sentence starters for opinions, visual aids during listening tasks)."</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and focused on interpreting opinions through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will revisit these same vocabulary words and grammatical structures within a written context, allowing students to deepen their understanding of community support issues by reading an article or report related to volunteer programs and government initiatives, further reinforcing the theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>